@* The booster pack from the app icon, as a one-colour outline.

   Drawn once here because two places show it now — the wordmark in the top bar and the centre tab
   on a phone — and path data copied into two files is path data that disagrees with itself after the
   first tweak.

   currentColor throughout, no literal colours: that is what lets the same drawing sit in near-black
   ink on a light bar, near-white on a dark one, and white inside the filled action tab, without any
   of the three saying anything about colour. The previous version of this mark was the app icon put
   through `filter: grayscale(1)`, which needed a second rule inverting it in dark because greyscale
   put the icon's indigo tile at 1.7:1 against the dark bar.

   Redrawn rather than scaled down from icon.svg, on the grounds favicon.svg gives for being its own
   drawing: the icon's pack is a FILLED white shape on an indigo tile, and a white pack is invisible
   on a light bar. At this size its tear-strip dashes and the diamond's outline also turn to mush, so
   the strip is one line and the diamond a solid. The 8-degree tilt is kept — it is what stops the
   shape reading as a plain rectangle.

   A 24 viewBox with a 1.8 stroke, matching the other tab-bar glyphs exactly, so the mark carries the
   same weight as its neighbours at the same rendered size. Callers size it in CSS where they want it
   to track a type scale, and with Size where they want pixels like the icons beside it. *@
<svg class="@Class" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="@Size" height="@Size" focusable="false"
     aria-hidden="true">
    <g transform="rotate(-8 12 12)">
        @if (Filled)
        {
            @* THE SAME PACK, SOLID.
               ==========================================================================
               One path and one fill, with the tear strip and the diamond knocked out of it by
               evenodd rather than drawn over the top. They have to be holes: this mark paints in
               currentColor precisely so it can sit on any ground, and a strip painted in the bar's
               own colour would be this file deciding what that ground is — which is the failure
               the greyscale-filter version had, and the reason the mark became markup.

               The outer subpath is the same rounded rect the outline draws, written longhand
               because a <rect> cannot carry holes.

               The strip is INSET from both edges where the outline's runs the full width. Full
               width cuts a solid pack clean in two and reads as two shapes stacked; stopping short
               of the corners reads as a perforation across one. *@
            <path fill="currentColor" fill-rule="evenodd"
                  d="M9.3 3.45h5.4a2.25 2.25 0 0 1 2.25 2.25v12.6a2.25 2.25 0 0 1-2.25 2.25H9.3a2.25 2.25 0 0 1-2.25-2.25V5.7A2.25 2.25 0 0 1 9.3 3.45z
                     M8.55 6.9h6.9v1.5h-6.9z
                     M12 12.45l2.63 2.63L12 17.7l-2.63-2.62z" />
        }
        else
        {
            <rect x="7.05" y="3.45" width="9.9" height="17.1" rx="2.25"
                  fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            @* A fifth of the way down the pack, where the icon puts it: lower reads as a window
               rather than a sealed pack. *@
            <path d="M7.05 7.65h9.9" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" />
            <path d="M12 12.45L14.63 15.08L12 17.7L9.37 15.08z" fill="currentColor" />
        }
    </g>
</svg>

@code {
    /// <summary>Class on the svg, so a caller can size or hide it from the stylesheet.</summary>
    [Parameter] public string? Class { get; set; }

    /// <summary>
    /// Rendered size in px. Only a default — a CSS rule on <see cref="Class"/> outranks the
    /// attribute, which is how the top bar sizes this in rem instead.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public int Size { get; set; } = 24;

    /// <summary>
    /// Draw the pack solid instead of as an outline.
    ///
    /// For the wordmark, and so far only the wordmark. The outline is a line drawing at the same
    /// weight as the line drawings on the nav buttons beside it, and once those buttons had marks
    /// of their own the name stopped reading as the name — it was the same construction as its
    /// neighbours, minus a border. Solid makes it a different KIND of mark rather than a larger
    /// one of the same kind, which is what size alone could not buy.
    ///
    /// A parameter and not a second component: it is the same drawing at the same coordinates, and
    /// the two forms have to go on agreeing about the tilt, the strip and the diamond. Two files
    /// would be the thing this file exists to prevent.
    ///
    /// The centre tab on a phone keeps the outline. It is already white on a filled circle, so it
    /// has no legibility to gain, and a solid mark there would read as a pressed state on the one
    /// control in the app that writes something.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter] public bool Filled { get; set; }
}
